<!--
  /overview — the introduction. What SUI IS, in plain words, and a page you can
  look at.

  This page used to open with "The UI framework agents write" and then explain
  element mode, compile-away tags, and four mechanisms. On the FIRST page. It
  never once mentioned that we ship a component library.
-->
<column class="doc" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">Introduction</p>
  <h1>SUI</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A batteries-included framework for building web pages. It ships a component
    library, a design system, layout, theming, animation, and state — so a page
    is markup, and nothing else has to be assembled first.
  </p>

  <h2>Install it</h2>
  <code-block code={code.install}></code-block>
  <p>
    That is the whole setup. No config file, no CSS framework to pick, no
    component library to install on top — the components are part of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Write a page</h2>
  <p>
    The tags below are the component library. They are the ones you already know
    from HTML, plus the ones HTML is missing.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.page}></code-block>

  <h2>Here it is</h2>
  <p>Same markup, running:</p>

  <column gap="4" sx="p:6 bg:muted border:border radius:lg">
    <h3>Team</h3>

    <combobox placeholder="Filter by role">
      <option value="eng">Engineering</option>
      <option value="design">Design</option>
    </combobox>

    <card sx="p:4" motion:hover={{ scale: 'spring:gentle' }}>
      <row gap="3" align="center">
        <avatar></avatar>
        <column gap="1">
          <strong>Ada Lovelace</strong>
          <p sx="fg:muted-foreground text:sm">Engineering</p>
        </column>
        <badge tone="success">Active</badge>
      </row>
    </card>

    <card sx="p:4" motion:hover={{ scale: 'spring:gentle' }}>
      <row gap="3" align="center">
        <avatar></avatar>
        <column gap="1">
          <strong>Grace Hopper</strong>
          <p sx="fg:muted-foreground text:sm">Engineering</p>
        </column>
        <badge tone="muted">Away</badge>
      </row>
    </card>

    <row gap="2">
      <button motion:press={{ scale: 'press' }}>Invite</button>
      <button variant="ghost">Export</button>
    </row>
  </column>

  <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">
    Hover a card. Change the theme in the top right — every colour, shadow and
    radius above moves with it, because they all come from the same system.
  </p>

  <h2>What comes with it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>48 components.</strong> Buttons, dialogs, combobox, tabs, toasts,
      tables — accessible, keyboard-complete, and themed.
      <a href="/elements">The library →</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Layout.</strong> <code>&lt;row&gt;</code>,
      <code>&lt;column&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;grid&gt;</code>. No flexbox to
      remember. <a href="/layout">Layout →</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Styling and themes.</strong> A token system and nine themes, with
      no CSS to write. <a href="/styling">Styling →</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Animation.</strong> One directive, springs included, respects
      reduced-motion. <a href="/motion">Animation →</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>State.</strong> Signals — fine-grained, no virtual DOM.
      <a href="/reactivity">State →</a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    And it is small: <strong>9.4&nbsp;kB gzipped</strong>, with performance in
    the same class as Solid.
  </p>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/getting-started">Installation →</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/why">Why SUI</a>
  </row>
</column>
